From a5f7ae6d0b67b5d106dcccc54acacb9c7b84acd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Hunt <dhuntlinux@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 11:55:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Start a recursive search when the user types in the window

Thunar only searches when the user explicitly asks for it with Ctrl+F.
Typing a character into a view instead triggers the GTK views' own
interactive search, which merely moves the cursor to the next matching
name in the current folder. Nautilus starts a real (recursive) search
right away, which is a much more useful default for a file manager.

Add thunar_window_type_ahead_search(), a key-press-event handler on
ThunarWindow which turns a plain printable keystroke into a search
query by pushing it into the location bar's search entry. The regular
Thunar search machinery takes it from there, so "Include subfolders"
(misc-recursive-search) keeps deciding how deep the search goes, and
Escape still cancels it.

The handler is connected after thunar_window_location_toolbar_create(),
so the custom action (UCA) shortcuts checked there keep their priority.
It refuses to act on modified keys, non-printable or whitespace keys,
keys bound to an accelerator, '/' and '~' (which open the location
bar), and any key press that happens while a GtkEditable has the focus,
so the location bar and the rename dialog are unaffected.

The feature can be switched off with the new boolean preference
misc-type-ahead-search, exposed as "Search as you type" in
Preferences -> Advanced -> Search. It defaults to enabled.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
